s/printf/the wheel/, no? Both GNOME and KDE have extensive gettext-
based translation systems that handle upwards of 50 languages,
including all the vagaries of pluralization.

>     pline("osi", _("%{Nxt} is welded to your %{np3}!"),
>           P_o(uwep), P_s(body_part(HAND)), P_i(bimanual(uwep)? 2 : 1));

Don't forget that your changes according to the number and gender
of the arms / tentacles, so you need to change that too, and
similarly is welded changes according to the gender of the thing
is applies to.

Hence the adage: don't substitute stuff into a translated string.
Formulate a single thought and translate it. Unfortunately
objects and dungeon features and bits of the player interact in the
strings produced by nethack so extensively, that you can't realistically
take the product space and translate that. Hence, you need some kind
of engine to do _all_ the tranlsation of all the parts of speech
found in the sentences produced by nethack. And that covers a lot more
than uwep, body part, etc.

Something like

pline("osi", _"%{Nxt} %{vPP} %{ppn} %{np3}!",
  P_o(uwep),P_vb("is welded",uwep->grammarinfo),
  P_ppn("to your",body_part(HAND)->grammarinfo,bimanual(uwep) ? 2 : 1),
  P_s(body_part(HAND)),
  ...
  );

is more like what you're likely to end up with, and you're still stuck
with word order (la spada e agglutinata alle mane sue vs. alle sue mane,
both legal sentences with different stress).

All in all, a major undertaking to get it right.

